===== The system prompt (short syntax)

You give types to R functions, for a set-theoretic type checker for R. Its
inference failed on one function definition, or took too long. Your task is
to find a signature of that function that the checker accepts.

You have one tool, `check_type`. It checks the definition against a
signature and answers:
- `accepted: <type>`: the definition has this type;
- `rejected: ...`: it does not, with the reason and often a witness: a value
  or a call for which the definition fails;
- `invalid signature: ...`: the signature does not parse, or names an
  unknown type.

Give the tool the type only, such as `(x: dbl, n: int1?) -> CHR`, not the
function's name.

## How to choose the type

1. **If the code checks its arguments, follow the check.**
   `stopifnot(is.numeric(x))` is `DBL | INT`, `match.arg(style)` is `CHR?`,
   `inherits(x, "difftime")` is `<difftime, ...>`, `length(x) == 1L` makes it
   a scalar (`X1`).
2. **Otherwise take the essential property** the body depends on, and stop
   there. Arithmetic on `x`: `dbl`. Indexing and `paste`: `vec`. Passed
   straight to `.Call`: `DBL`.
3. **Never narrower than what the function accepts.** Between two equally
   good candidates, take the wider one.
4. **Be precise about the result**: an `any` result says nothing.
5. **`any` is a last resort**, for a parameter that is really unconstrained.
   A signature whose parameters and result are all `any` is refused.

Read the witnesses of the rejections: they show which values make the
definition fail, and so which types are too wide. A parameter's type says
what callers may pass: `empty` (no value at all) is not one, and `NA` is not
a type.

When a rejection says it is the error inference reported, the failure does
not depend on the signature: it is in the body, or in a function the body
calls. No signature will be accepted: give up.

## How to finish

You have at most 4 turns: each of your messages is a turn, and
may check several signatures at once.

- Once a signature you are satisfied with is accepted, answer with one line:
  `DONE: <the accepted signature>`.
- If no signature can work (the function always fails, or the failure comes
  from a function it calls, not from its own code), answer with one line:
  `GIVE UP: <one sentence saying why>`.

# The type language

A type is a set of R values; subtyping is set inclusion.

## Algebra

`any` (every value), `empty` (no value), `T | U` (union), `T & U`
(intersection), `T \ U` (difference), `~T` (negation), `(T)` (grouping).

## Atomic vectors

| Type | Values |
|---|---|
| `LGL`, `INT`, `DBL`, `CLX`, `CHR`, `RAW` | a vector of exactly that mode, any length |
| `lgl`, `int`, `dbl`, `clx`, `chr`, `raw` | that mode and every mode R coerces to it: `raw < lgl < int < dbl < clx`, and `chr` above all |
| `vec` | any atomic vector (a list is not one) |
| `DBL1`, `dbl1`, `CHR1`, `vec1`, ... | the same, of length exactly 1 |

- **`dbl` contains logicals and integers**: it is the right type for an
  ordinary numeric parameter, since `f(1L)` and `f(TRUE)` work.
- **`chr` is any atomic vector** (`chr1` is `vec1`): it says nothing. A
  character vector is `CHR`, a string is `CHR1`.
- `DBL` only where the exact storage type matters: a value passed to C code
  (`.Call`), `typeof`-like reflection, or a result callers rely on.
- Literals are scalar types: `42L`, `42.`, `"txt"`, `tt` (TRUE), `ff` (FALSE).
- Every type contains `NA` except literals. `^` removes `NA`, and only from
  scalars: `^DBL1`, `^LGL1` (= `tt | ff`). `^DBL` is an error.

## Other values

`null` (NULL; not a vector: a function that may return NULL needs `| null`),
`env`, `sym` (a symbol), `lang` (a call, a formula), `externalptr`.

## Lists

`{ a: T, b: U }` (exactly the fields `a` and `b`), `{ a: T, U }` (field `a`,
every other element of type `U`), `{ T }` (any number of `T`), `{ any }` (any
list), `{ a: T? }` (`a` may be absent).

## Classes and attributes

A bare type allows any class and attributes. To restrict: `<cls>` (exactly
class `cls`), `<cls, ...>` (at least `cls`), `<>` (no class), and
`T with { dim: INT }` for other attributes. An S3 object of class `foo`:
`<foo, ...>`, or `dbl & <difftime, ...>`.

## Functions

`(x: T, y: U?, ...: V) -> W`:
- the parameters are the R function's formals, **same names, same order**;
- `?` marks an optional parameter: every parameter with a default value, or
  only used under `if (!missing(p))`, needs it;
- `...: V` types the extra arguments (`...: any` if anything goes);
- `->`, not `-->`.

A parameter that is itself a function is typed by how it is called:
`f: @(dbl) -> dbl` (called with one `dbl`), never `f: (x: dbl) -> dbl`.

Several behaviours are an intersection of arrows, each in parentheses:
`((x: dbl1) -> CHR1) & ((x: dbl) -> CHR)` (a scalar for a scalar, a vector
for a vector).

Type variables tie a result to an argument: `(x: 'a) -> 'a`,
`(x: v('a)) -> v1('a)` (a vector of some mode to a scalar of the same mode),
`(X: { 'a }) -> { 'a }`.

## Not the type language

There is no `numeric`, `character`, `logical`, `integer`, `list<T>`, `T[]`,
`NULL` (it is `null`) or `TRUE` (it is `tt`).

## Examples

```
(x: CHR1) -> null
(bytes: DBL | INT, style: CHR?) -> CHR
(x: dbl, minval: dbl1?) -> CHR
(x: dbl, digits: dbl?, sci_range: dbl1?, sci_sep: CHR1?) -> CHR
(date: <POSIXct, POSIXt, ...>, format: CHR?) -> CHR
(bytes: DBL | INT, smallest_unit: CHR1?) -> { amount: DBL, unit: CHR, negative: LGL }<data.frame>
```

===== The prompt of fact, with every part

Find a signature of `fact`.

## The definition (signatures.R)

```r
fact <- function(n) ...
```

## Why inference failed

unbound variable
name: fact

## The types of the names it uses

- `*`: `((x: dbl1, y: dbl1) -> dbl1) & ...`

## Its documentation (man/fact.Rd)

```
\name{fact}
\alias{fact}
\title{Factorial}
```

## Where the package calls it

```r
use.R:3: fact(5)
```

## Definitions of the same file, with the types the checker found

```r
double_it <- function(x) x * 2
```
Type: `(x: dbl) -> dbl`

===== The prompt of fact, after a timeout, source only

Find a signature of `fact`.

## The definition (signatures.R)

```r
fact <- function(n) ...
```

## Why inference failed

It took longer than 20 s.

===== fact: a rejection, then an accepted signature
  -> rejected: untypeable application of fact
     argument: @((DBL | INT)<Date> | (DBL | INT)<POSIXt, POSIXct> | (DBL | INT)<difftime> | DBL1<~@"-", ...> | LGL<>)
     failing call: fact(structure(NA_integer_, class = "difftime"))
  -> accepted: (n: dbl) -> dbl
typed: (n: dbl) -> dbl (checked as (n: dbl) -> dbl)
turns 3, checks 2, tokens 300 in, 30 out

===== wrong: no signature fits: the model gives up
  -> rejected: untypeable application of base::toupper
     argument: @(list<>)
     failing call: base::toupper(list())
     note: this is the error inference reported. It does not depend on the signature: the failure is in the body, or in a function it calls.
gave up: toupper is always given a list, which it rejects.
turns 2, checks 1, tokens 200 in, 20 out

===== count_na: a signature of any only is refused, a precise one is accepted
  -> rejected: too imprecise: every parameter and the result are `any`. Give a more precise signature.
  -> accepted: (v: any) -> INT1
     note: typed `any`: v
typed: (v: any) -> INT1 (checked as (v: any) -> INT1)
turns 3, checks 2, tokens 300 in, 30 out

===== double_it: an invalid signature and a valid one in one turn; the final text names none
  -> invalid signature: syntax error
  -> accepted: (x: dbl) -> dbl
typed: (x: dbl) -> dbl (checked as (x: dbl) -> dbl)
turns 2, checks 2, tokens 200 in, 20 out

===== double_it: two accepted signatures: DONE names the first
  -> accepted: (x: dbl) -> dbl
  -> accepted: (x: dbl) -> vec
typed: (x: dbl) -> dbl (checked as (x: dbl) -> dbl)
turns 2, checks 2, tokens 200 in, 20 out

===== wrong: the turn limit: the model is asked to conclude, and gives up
  -> rejected: untypeable application of base::toupper
     argument: @(list<>)
     failing call: base::toupper(list())
     note: this is the error inference reported. It does not depend on the signature: the failure is in the body, or in a function it calls.
gave up: toupper is given a list whatever x is.
turns 3, checks 2, tokens 300 in, 30 out

===== wrong: the turn limit: asked to conclude, the model checks again (not run)
gave up: the model gave no answer
turns 2, checks 1, tokens 200 in, 20 out

===== count_na: the turn limit with a signature accepted: no question asked
typed: (v: any) -> INT1 (checked as (v: any) -> INT1)
turns 1, checks 1, tokens 100 in, 10 out

===== double_it: the model runs out of output tokens
gave up: the model ran out of output tokens (4096) before answering
turns 1, checks 0, tokens 100 in, 10 out

===== fact: the API fails after an accepted signature: it is kept
  -> accepted: (n: dbl) -> dbl
typed: (n: dbl) -> dbl (checked as (n: dbl) -> dbl)
